<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhumij people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bhumij are an Adivasi people of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, living mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>. Speaking a Munda language closely related to Mundari, they traditionally farmed in the hills and forests of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chota-nagpur-plateau">Chota Nagpur</a> plateau. In 1832–1833 Bhumij of the Jungle Mahals rose under Ganga Narayan Singh against British-backed landlords and officials.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhumij-people.jpg" alt="Bhumij people" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sumita Roy Dutta · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Firkal_slowly_decaying_martial_dance_of_ChotaNagpur_of_Jharkhand_performed_at_Central_Park_Delhi_IMG_0934_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
